  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ARGENTINA Annual Action Programme 2023 - Supporting Agricultural Green Transition in Argentina</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Due to the prolonged economic crisis, combined with the impact of the COVID-19 crisis, Argentina has faced food insecurity and during 2020-2021 over 11[1] million people attended community kitchens; a significant part of Argentina&#xbf;s total 46 million population. Two years later, that number is still estimated between 9 and 10 million people, particularly affecting children.The government has implemented a variety of programs to fight food insecurity, typically through a traditional two-fold approach: i) assistance to the agricultural sector to promote food production; and, ii) support to food consumption and distribution among households in need. The Provincial Agricultural Services Programme (PROSAP) and ProHuerta are some of the long-lasting programs that contributed to the first objective; and, Plan Nacional de Seguridad Alimentaria, Abordaje Comunitario and Plan Argentina contra el Hambre (Argentina against Hunger), are examples of programs currently executed related to the second objective. The innovation proposed by this initiative is to implement a new methodology that will integrate several principles supported by the EU in the Farm to Fork Strategy and other policies promoting green transition into the management of Argentina&#xbf;s traditional food security operations. The initiative has been called the Sustainable Food Supply Network (SFSN) and has the purpose of creating a long lasting, scalable and sustainable grid between local small landholders and their communities while promoting greener and more sustainable agricultural practices and policies. Even though Argentina is a global exporter of some agricultural commodities obtained through extensive practices, such as soy and wheat, most producers are not big exporters. In fact, small-holder farmers account for more than 66% of agricultural productive units, and the percentage scales up to 80% in the Northern provinces. They are part of regional economies&#xbf; value chains who could, with the support of technical and financial assistance, implement innovation and more sustainable practices -and, certainly become suppliers of good quality food (such as fresh vegetables and fruits) for the community kitchens, their communities in general and the more vulner</narrative>
  </description>
